There is a major problem with casting spells, and I don't see how it can
be fixed without rethinking the rationale behind the magic system.

Has anyone ever been unable to cast a spell because their magic points
were too low?  Me neither.  By the time my character has a few levels
under his belt, which is when I start reading any spellbooks I have, the
magic points are up high enough so that they're never depleted.  So the
idea of magic points has limited usefulness.

Has anyone ever found a second spellbook for a spell they knew, and
relearned one that was too worn out to read?  Maybe twice I've been able
to make my knowledge of a spell keener.  So for all intents and
purposes, a spell is like a wand with half a dozen charges.  Use them
and they're gone.  The type or level of your character makes no
appreciable difference except for characters with low intelligence who
goof up.

The way it is weighted now makes spells a trivial part of the game,
which makes it unnecessarily hard for characters who rely on magic.  A
fairer system would be one where any spell could be cast unlimited
times, but the "points" actually had some meaning; for instance, a spell
like "detect food (1)" might mean that once you cast it, you had to wait
1 x 1000 moves before casting it again.  (Or maybe casting any other
spell again.)  Point values could be increased so that you were likely
to only be able to cast some spells once per dungeon level or once per 2
levels.

IMHO, this would greatly add to the strategy aspect of the game.  It's
ridiculous to use up a spell like force bolt or fireball early in the
game because the character had to cast it at a few killer bees or hill
orcs, then explore 40+ more levels unable to cast it.  Either the
character is weak and uses the spells very early, or is powerful and
never bothers to cast any spells because of the possibility of goofing
up.

A rethink of the whole spell system would make the game more balanced.
Go for it!

